\description{
Draws the network bin by bin over the measurement grid and writes the
frames to an animated GIF or a video. The grid is the same four arguments
every measuring verb takes, so an animation shows exactly what
\code{\link[=snapshots]{snapshots()}} tabulates and what \code{plot(dn, type = "snapshots")} draws as a
filmstrip, with the bins joined by motion.
}
\details{
\strong{Frames.} Each bin is drawn \code{tween} times. Between one bin and the next
the vertices glide to their new positions, a tie that is about to appear
fades in and one that is about to vanish fades out, and a vertex whose
measure changes grows or shrinks. Under \code{ease = "dwell"}, the default,
the motion follows the smoothstep curve, so each bin holds still before
it starts to change and the bins can be read one by one. Under
\code{ease = "continuous"} nothing holds still: positions follow a Catmull-Rom
spline through the bins, so a vertex moving across several bins traces
one smooth path, and fades are linear. \code{tween = 1} gives one frame per
bin with hard cuts. A film that feels episodic usually has a grid whose
bins do not overlap; a sliding window, \code{step} smaller than \code{window},
smooths the data itself, since a tie then persists across several bins.

\strong{Layouts.} Under every layout but \code{"relaxed"} a vertex keeps one position
for the whole animation, so the only thing that moves is the ties; those
are the layouts to read structure from. \code{"spring"}, the default, lays out
the union of every frame once with \code{cograph::layout_spring()}, so pairs
that met often sit close. \code{"circle"} and \code{"oval"} are rings, in vertex
order. \code{"groups"} puts each partition on its own ring and needs a network
built with \verb{groups = }. \code{"relaxed"} lays each frame out again, seeded from
the previous one and held near it by \code{max_displacement} and
\code{anchor_strength}, then smooths every vertex's path with a centred
triangular kernel over one bin each side; clusters can form and dissolve
without vertices jumping, and no vertex moves further than
\code{max_displacement} between consecutive bins. Each relaxed frame is framed
by its active vertices: a vertex that is absent or has no tie in the bin
is held at the border of that frame rather than drifting outward under
repulsion, which would shrink the picture. \code{layout_args} tunes the spring
layout for both. A data frame with columns \code{name} (or \code{node}), \code{x} and
\code{y} fixes the positions yourself.

\strong{What is drawn.} Tie width follows weight on one scale fixed across the
whole animation, so a tie of the same weight has the same width in a quiet
frame and a busy one. With \code{tie_states = TRUE} a tie forming during a
transition is dotted and green, one persisting is solid and grey, and one
dissolving is dashed and vermilion, so the distinction survives without
colour. With \code{measure} given, node size follows that measure, again on
one scale across every frame, with the area of the circle proportional to
the measure's position in its range; see the argument for the three forms
it takes.

\strong{Absence and idleness.} A vertex that is not present in a bin, under
declared vertex activity or observation bounds, is drawn as \code{absent}
says: faded in place, parked out of sight at the edge of the layout and
gliding in when it arrives and out when it leaves, or hidden in place. A
network built without vertex activity has every vertex present in every
bin; \code{set_vertex_spells(dn, "ties")} declares each vertex present from
its first tie to its last. A vertex that is present but has no tie in a
bin is drawn as \code{isolates} says. With \code{timeline = TRUE} a strip under the
network shows the grid with a marker at the current time and the key to
the drawing.

\strong{Files.} The extension of \code{file} chooses the encoder: \code{.gif} is written
by the gifski package, \code{.mp4} and \code{.webm} by the av package. A video needs
even pixel dimensions. Writing the file is the point of the verb, but the
tidy bin table is still what comes back, so the animation can be described
without opening it.
}
